19 of 19 valid mutants killed (19 attempted)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The first run found five gaps. All five were real and all five are closed.

FRONTEND, first run: 9 of 12. Three survivors, and all three were ONE defect.
`loading` and `failed` were the same state -- the absence of a value -- so the
panel said *the evidence could not be loaded* for as long as a fetch was in
flight, which is false at the moment it is shown. With the two merged, the
hook's two mechanisms (clear on the way in, set on failure) each satisfied the
test alone, so neither could be shown load-bearing and a mutant removing either
survived. The states are now NAMED -- `loading`, `failed`, `null`, an account --
and `undefined` no longer appears in the contract.

  Of the three, one was a user-visible defect found by nothing else. A test
  asserts an outcome; it cannot assert that two states which produce the same
  outcome on the fixture are different states.

FRONTEND, second run: 12 of 13. One survivor left: removing the clear on the
way in. The loading test only MOUNTED, and the hook's initial state is already
loading, so the clear could not be told from the initial value. Killed by a test
that changes the hour mid-flight -- `anom-015`'s rule for an ordering guard, in
another costume: a guard against stale state is killed only by a test that
supplies stale state on purpose.

BACKEND, first run: 17 of 19, and one of the two "survivors" was not one.
`unclaimed = [` -> `unclaimed = [] or [` is EQUIVALENT: `[] or [x]` is `[x]` in
Python. The mutation table's own validity check does not catch that, because the
file parses and the text differs. Replaced with a mutant on the comprehension's
filter.

The other was real: `min(m.started_at for m in members)` -> `members[0]`
survived because the builder emits a group's items in time order, so first IS
earliest on every fixture. Killed by a test that reverses two items on purpose.

BACKEND, second run: 19 of 19.
